WEBVTT

a01
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.952
So. There are two honest ways to make a model smaller, and you will have both by the end of this. Train a small model with a big one's help. Or take the big one and cut real pieces out of it.

a02
00:00:11.902 --> 00:00:22.645
Let's start with a handwritten seven. What does the dataset tell us about it? One thing. Class seven is certain, every other class is impossible. That's the whole label.

a03
00:00:22.995 --> 00:00:36.308
Now ask an already-trained teacher the same question. And honestly? At first it looks like the same fact, written less tidily. 0.93 on the seven, and the rest looks like noise.

a04
00:00:36.658 --> 00:00:48.094
But it isn't. Eight more bars are not zero — they're only too short to see. So redraw the same ten numbers on a log axis, where each gridline is ten times smaller than the one above.

a05
00:00:48.444 --> 00:01:02.237
And now the teacher is readable. It's telling you a seven resembles a one about a hundred times more than it resembles an eight. Nothing in the dataset ever says that. That ordering has a name: dark knowledge.

a06
00:01:02.887 --> 00:01:11.150
Right. Six raw scores, straight from the teacher. Nothing from here on changes them — the only thing we change is the number we divide them by.

a07
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:23.523
At τ = 1 this is the ordinary softmax. One class takes 0.904. The other five lie flat against the axis, and a gradient can barely feel them.

a08
00:01:23.873 --> 00:01:38.082
So turn the dial. At τ = 4 the winner drops to 0.368. And look at the wrong answers — the gaps between them are now wide enough to carry a gradient. That's the whole trick.

a09
00:01:38.432 --> 00:01:47.254
Push on to τ = 10 and you've gone too far. Every class sits near 0.17, and the teacher's ordering has dissolved.

a09b
00:01:47.604 --> 00:01:55.427
So come back. Two to four, for classifiers. You're trading the teacher's confidence against its uncertainty, and you want both.

a10
00:01:56.377 --> 00:02:16.239
Now the part that's easy to skip, and expensive to skip. Raise the temperature and this term's gradient collapses. From τ = 1 to τ = 2 it shrinks 3.3 times. From 2 to 4, another 3.8. Why? Two halvings, multiplied. That's the square.

a11
00:02:17.189 --> 00:02:36.736
So multiply the KL term by τ², and it holds still. At τ = 2, a raw KL of 0.0685 becomes 0.274. At τ = 4, 0.0174 becomes 0.278. The whole loss barely moves.

a12
00:02:37.686 --> 00:02:52.718
Here's one training step. The same batch goes into two models. And one hard requirement: they must share a tokenizer, because position 4,271 of the student's logits has to mean the same token as the teacher's.

a13
00:02:53.068 --> 00:03:05.895
The teacher is frozen. Evaluation mode, gradients switched off, forward pass inside no_grad. Get that wrong and you build a tape for a model you will never update — and pay memory for it.

a14
00:03:06.245 --> 00:03:18.438
The KL term compares the two full distributions. It grades every class at once, not just the right one. And notice which model is written first. The teacher. That's forward KL.

a15
00:03:18.788 --> 00:03:31.794
Cross-entropy grades one number: the probability on the correct token. Then α weighs the two, and they collapse into a single number. 0.439. That's what backward() is called on.

a16
00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:41.287
And the gradient comes back to the student only. The teacher has nothing to fill in. It never learns a thing — which is exactly why you froze it.

a17
00:03:41.937 --> 00:03:59.639
Now a problem the classifier never had. Train a student only on the teacher's text, and it learns only in states the teacher would visit. Its first mistake at inference lands it somewhere it has never been. That's exposure error. So let the student write first.

a18
00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:10.346
Sampling isn't differentiable, so nothing tries to differentiate through it. Once those four tokens exist, they're fixed data. Exactly like a batch read from disk.

a19
00:04:10.696 --> 00:04:23.518
Now re-run the student over prompt plus completion, this time with gradient. And run the frozen teacher over that same fixed sequence. Once. One forward pass is the entire added cost.

a20
00:04:23.868 --> 00:04:42.823
Compare the two at every token — with the student written first. That's reverse KL. Forward KL charges a sharp student 1.59 for the mode it dropped, and only 0.36 for hedging. Reverse flips it: 0.63 against 0.91.

a21
00:04:43.173 --> 00:05:04.015
And the gradient goes back to the student alone. Does it pay? Same checkpoint, same maths exam: reinforcement learning reached 67.6, in 17,920 GPU hours. On-policy distillation reached 74.4. In 1,800.

a22
00:05:04.965 --> 00:05:22.198
But be honest about when not to do this. A stronger teacher can give you a worse student. It cannot represent the function it's being shown, so it cannot optimise toward it either. Bigger teacher is not automatically better. That's the capacity gap.

a23
00:05:23.148 --> 00:05:40.301
Right. Now the other family: pruning. On the left, one nn.Linear weight matrix. On the right, four meters — how much of it you deleted, then bytes read, time per token, and file size.

a24
00:05:40.651 --> 00:05:51.712
Rank every weight by size, and zero the smallest half. Half the numbers in that tensor are gone. Fifty per cent sparsity. Now watch the other three meters.

a25
00:05:52.062 --> 00:06:07.211
Nothing. Bytes read, unchanged — the tensor is the same shape, so the memory system still reads every zero. Time per token, unchanged — a dense matrix multiply cheerfully multiplies by all of them.

a26
00:06:07.561 --> 00:06:17.450
So say it plainly. Unstructured sparsity is a research metric. To make real hardware faster, you have to change the shape, not the values.

a27
00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:31.764
So change the shape. Structured pruning starts by scoring every output row — and a good score uses the activations reaching that row, not just |w|. A small weight meeting a huge activation still matters.

a28
00:06:32.114 --> 00:06:44.947
The three weakest rows go, and the rest slide up. Nine rows became six. And this time the shape itself changed — every runtime feels that. No special kernel, no sparse format.

a29
00:06:45.297 --> 00:06:56.120
But look at the plot on the right. The cut costs accuracy immediately, and the child now scores well below the parent it was carved out of. So is that it? No.

a30
00:06:56.470 --> 00:07:15.313
You heal. Keep training the child, with the unpruned parent as its teacher. Minitron drops the label term completely here — alpha = 0, teacher only. Most of the loss comes back, and the model stays permanently smaller. Prune first, distil second.

a31
00:07:15.963 --> 00:07:29.996
There's a middle ground, and silicon designers made it exist. 2:4 sparsity: in every group of four weights, exactly two must be zero. Fifty per cent sparsity, in a layout the chip can predict.

a32
00:07:30.346 --> 00:07:40.866
Store only the survivors, and give each one a 2-bit index saying which of the four slots it came from. Two bits. That's all it takes to name one of four.

a32b
00:07:41.216 --> 00:07:57.415
And here is what that index buys. Sparse tensor cores on Ampere and later read it, fetch only the survivors, and skip the discarded half entirely. Twice the arithmetic per clock. So the datasheet promises 2.00×.

a33
00:07:57.765 --> 00:08:07.548
What people actually measure is smaller. 1.23× on BERT-Large, and about 1.4× on Llama-2-7B end to end.

a33b
00:08:07.898 --> 00:08:16.640
So 1.2 to 1.45 is the honest range — and only when the batch or the prompt is long enough to pay for the sparse kernel.

a34
00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:40.733
Now go and build it. Distil that 360M model into a 135M one. Then hunt for the uncomfortable number. 360 to 135 is only 2.7 times. The other 2 times is bytes — one byte a weight instead of two. Lesson 07 takes that much further.

